      "answer": {
        "answer": "NO_MATCH",
        "finding": "Compared three plausible candidates at pinned commits, reading implementation and each repository's MIT license notice. None forcibly stops an already running, noncooperative callback. sindresorhus/p-limit:index.js (a8a6fbec4e0e866d6d779b10889bb4f5567e70eb): pLimit({concurrency: 1, rejectOnClear: true}).clearQueue() rejects only queued entries; the default rejectOnClear:false discards them without settling their promises. run() invokes the callback and awaits its result; running callbacks are untouched, and rejection releases the slot even if side effects continue. sindresorhus/p-queue:source/index.ts (180ab9e25cd10b6f548767d7176076b50d25e188): new PQueue({concurrency: 1}).add(callback, {signal}) passes the signal to the callback, then races its operation against an abort rejection. Aborting running work rejects the outer promise and releases queue accounting through finally; it does not terminate the callback, so another task can start while its side effects continue. Waiting aborts remove work with the default PriorityQueue; custom queue removal is best-effort. pause() blocks future starts; clear() replaces waiting work without settling its promises. The relevant add()/clear() implementation was read in the subsequent source window at offset 12000. This TypeScript package exports compiled dist/index.js as ESM, requires Node >=20, and uses eventemitter3 and p-timeout. sindresorhus/p-throttle:index.js (a0d20bf8c1a5f0067b1dce529b1ff013878591d3): pThrottle({limit: 1, interval: 1000, signal})(callback) cancels timers and rejects delayed calls on abort. execute() invokes the callback, adopts its result, and removes its timer entry without awaiting completion; abort has no control over already running work. strict and weight alter scheduling, not termination, and this limiter does not cap running concurrency. All three notices require preserving copyright and permission notices and disclaim warranties; dependency/component licensing was not audited. Searches included JavaScript and TypeScript without restrictive language or license filters. These are source-based findings, not independent testing. No upstream code was executed, installed, modified, or published. Under your constraints, promise settlement and abort signaling cannot guarantee cessation of arbitrary callback side effects."
